TI  - A survey of unit test case generation methods
PY  - 2026
T2  - Information and Software Technology
VL  - 196
C7  - 108142
DO  - 10.1016/j.infsof.2026.108142
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035241641?origin=resultslist
AD  - School of Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence, Zhengzhou University, 100 Science Avenue, Henan Province, Zhengzhou, 450001, China
AD  - School of Management, Lanzhou University, 222 Tianshui South Road, Gansu Province, Lanzhou, 730000, China
AB  - Context: Unit testing plays a vital role in the software development process. However, manually creating unit test cases is tedious and error-prone, making automated generation a persistent research focus. Various approaches have been proposed, but each has its own strengths and limitations. Objectives: This paper systematically reviews recent advances in automated unit test case generation over the past five years. It aims to statistically analyze the research trends of various methodologies, summarize the enhancements and technical specifics of mainstream techniques, with particular emphasis on search-based and deep learning-based approaches, and identify key challenges from multiple perspectives, ultimately proposing promising directions for future research. Methods: We collected 171 relevant papers published in the last five years, conducted a quantitative analysis, and selected 67 high-quality studies for detailed assessment. Results: We found that, over the past five years, search-based methods have received the most research attention, accounting for approximately 54% of the analyzed studies. Since 2023, approaches based on large language models (LLMs) have emerged and quickly gained popularity, representing about 24% of the studies. Some approaches that combine search-based methods with LLMs have achieved better results, showing a growing trend toward combining multiple techniques. Conclusion: Although automated unit test case generation has made substantial advances, challenges remain in test quality, execution efficiency, and real-world applicability. Future research could continue exploring innovations in generation methods and performance optimization, for example, by leveraging the semantic understanding capabilities of LLMs to mitigate the limitations of search-based, symbolic execution, and similar methods, or by combining multiple methods to fully exploit the advantages of each approach. In addition, evaluating test generation methods across a broader range of programming languages is essential to better assess their generalizability. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
