AD  - Duke University, Durham, NC, United States
AB  - Human skills, such as teamwork, communication, and ethical reasoning, are crucial in STEM education and the modern workforce. They enable students and professionals to work effectively in diverse teams, translate technical solutions into user-friendly applications, and navigate the complex ethical considerations that often arise in technological innovation. Despite widespread consensus on the importance of human skills, companies face a significant workforce gap. This article describes the design, implementation, and evaluation of an undergraduate software engineering course that embeds human skills and Oxford-style debates directly into its technical and collaborative learning activities by leveraging large language models’ capabilities to address integration barriers (e.g., time constraints, resource scarcity, lack of scaffolding). A mixed-methods evaluation, including pre-/post-surveys and qualitative analysis, examined changes in students’ perceptions of their interest, relevance, learning potential, satisfaction, and transferability of human skills. Results indicate consistently positive interest, relevance, learning potential, satisfaction, and transferability of human skills, suggesting that structured discussion and emphasis on collaboration can reinforce both technical learning and professional skill development. These findings underscore the feasibility of integrating human skills training into technical curricula to prepare students for the collaborative and ethically complex realities of modern software engineering. The implications of embedding human skills training within computing curricula are discussed. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s)
