AB  - Managing user demands from heterogeneous sources is critical for reducing redundant software development. However, traditional methods, such as manual analysis and keyword-based NLP tools, struggle to synthesize multi-source feedback due to data heterogeneity, noise, and contextual complexity. This paper proposes a novel framework integrating Large Language Models (LLMs) with multiple Agents to address these challenges. First, we design a preprocessing pipeline to normalize unstructured inputs into a unified corpus, resolving cross-source semantic inconsistencies. Second, we constructed a four-level Agent architecture to achieve requirement sorting and evaluation, including multi-source heterogeneous data standardization, requirement parsing, requirement function conflict resolution, and requirement function priority ranking. Compared with traditional methods, this architecture can handle more complex and diverse requirement sources, and the requirement priority ranking is more automated. Finally, the system proposed in this paper was evaluated via expert voting and comparative evaluation. Data experiments show that the effective accuracy of requirements reaches 80.4%, and the average recall rate is 79.7%. The experimental results indicate that this system can improve the quality of final implementable requirements and save R&D costs through automated processing of effective requirements.  © 2025 The Authors.
