AB  - The rapid integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Large Language Models (LLMs) into the Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) promises significant transformations, primarily driving enhancements in efficiency, quality, and time-to-market. However, quantifying the economic return on investment (ROI) from these technologies often remains challenging due to the highly contextual and non-linear nature of software projects. This paper proposes a quantitative framework designed for software projects to estimate AI-driven effort savings. The framework systematically maps projected percentage savings derived from AI automation across major SDLC phases, incorporates project-specific contextual risk factors that modulate these savings, and utilizes elements of existing software effort estimation (SEE) models, such as COCOMO II, FP, to generate a reliable AI-adjusted effort estimate. By defining potential savings with supporting evidence and detailing critical contextual influences, this model provides a structured, data-driven approach for organizations to forecast the measurable economic impact of AI integration.  © COPYRIGHT SPIE.
