TI  - SLMFL: Structural and semantic fusion for fault localization based on Small Language Models
PY  - 2026
T2  - Information and Software Technology
VL  - 196
C7  - 108143
DO  - 10.1016/j.infsof.2026.108143
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035247987?origin=resultslist
AD  - School of Software, Nanchang Hangkong University, Nanchang, 330063, China
AD  - Software Testing and Evaluation Center, Nanchang Hangkong University, Nanchang, 330063, China
AB  - Context: Software Fault Localization (SFL) is a fundamental technique in software debugging and plays a key role in improving the efficiency of software development and maintenance. Large Language Models (LLMs) have pioneered a test-free paradigm for SFL. However, the practical application of these models is severely impeded by their prohibitive computational costs. Furthermore, relying on external APIs for code processing raises serious privacy concerns. These limitations indicate the need for a lightweight, privacy-preserving and easily deployable alternative to replace SFL methods based on LLMs. Objective: To address these challenges, this paper proposes SLMFL, a lightweight fault localization framework based on Small Language Models (SLMs). To enhance the limited semantic understanding ability of SLMs, SLMFL fuses structural and semantic knowledge by a multi-task learning adapter. This adapter introduces line-level Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) node type prediction as an auxiliary task, integrating structural information with semantic features to significantly enhance structural awareness. Additionally, a hybrid loss function is designed to synergize classification and ranking objectives. Method: Experiments are performed on the Defects4J benchmark with 10-fold cross-validation to ensure the robustness of the results. We evaluate the effectiveness of SLMFL by comparing it with state-of-the-art baselines and traditional SBFL/MBFL methods, using metrics such as Top-N, Mean First Rank (MFR), and Mean Average Rank (MAR). Result: SLMFL outperforms the baseline methods in Top-1 and other metrics. SLMFL-3B localizes 105 faults at Top-1, outperforming the LLMAO-16B by 19.3% with less than 20% of the parameter size, while reducing inference time by 91.6% and peak VRAM to 8.5 GB. Additionally, the model demonstrates strong cross-version generalization on the unseen Defects4J 2.0.0 dataset. Conclusion: SLMFL provides a lightweight and efficient solution for SFL. This study empirically demonstrates that augmenting SLMs with fine-grained structural supervision (AST) enables them to outperform much larger LLMs in SFL. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
