TI  - LLM-Driven Agent-Based Evaluation Framework for Occupant-Centric HVAC Control Considering Individual Differences and Movement Behavior
PY  - 2025
T2  - BuildSys 2025 - Proceedings of the 2025 the 12th ACM International Conference on Systems for Energy-Efficient Buildings, Cities, and Transportation
SP  - 376
EP  - 383
DO  - 10.1145/3736425.3772325
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105022979809?origin=resultslist
AD  - The University of Osaka, Suita, Japan
AB  - The gap between predicted and measured Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning (HVAC) performance often stems from unmodeled inter-individual thermal sensations and occupant movement. Conventional assessments rely on fixed occupancy and population-average indices, providing no reproducible basis for fair, occupant-centric policy comparison. We propose an evaluation framework that couples Large Language Model (LLM)-driven agent-based modeling with Building Performance Simulation (BPS), thereby internalizing subjective sensations and behaviors. The framework comprises agent, control, orchestration, and environment layers that execute observation, response, control, environment update, and action application in a unified loop. In a case study (five rooms, 50 occupants, 10 hours), we compared three policies: s0 (global fixed), s1 (per-room fixed), and s2 (per-room multi-armed bandit) that explicitly leverages agent-derived thermal votes and the movement-induced occupancy redistribution exposed by the framework to adapt setpoints online. Relative to s0, s1 improved the comfort distribution by increasing neutral responses and reducing extreme dissatisfaction. s2 produced a generally decreasing cumulative discomfort, consistent with online learning, but yielded limited gains over short horizons. These findings demonstrate the framework's value as a decision-support basis and the applicability of LLM-driven ABM to HVAC.  © 2025 Copyright is held by the owner/author(s). Publication rights licensed to ACM.
