AB  - This article examines the implications of deploying large language models (LLM) in software development (SD), focusing on their impact on efficiency, security, and ethical considerations. The analysis highlights the potential of LLM to automate routine tasks, such as code generation, testing, and documentation creation, while contributing to resource optimization and productivity enhancement. The paper underscores associated risks, including the generation of vulnerable code, data leaks, and the unpredictable behavior of models. As part of the research, an experiment was conducted to evaluate the security and performance of LLM in real-world SD scenarios. The study utilized methods such as vulnerability analysis, threat modeling, and a comparative evaluation of different risk mitigation strategies. The findings confirm the hypothesis that integrating LLM requires a comprehensive approach encompassing technical, organizational, and ethical measures. The article concludes by emphasizing the importance of security standards and the adaptation of LLM to meet corporate needs. © 2026 Author(s).
