AB  - This work-in-progress paper explores how Large Language Models (LLMs) can elevate code quality in software engineering education and practice. While recent advancements show promise in improving code style, documentation, and design principle adherence, existing research is fragmented, making it difficult to identify best practices. A curated review of 50 papers, screened and categorized using Covidence, focuses on three critical dimensions: code styling, documentation generation, and alignment with design principles. Studies limited to performance metrics or debugging were excluded to maintain a targeted scope. Initial analysis reveals that, although LLMs generate readable code, they often lack context-aware support for robust design and well-structured documentation. This research aims to establish a taxonomy of LLM applications and deliver actionable guidelines for using LLMs to produce clean, maintainable code that aligns with both instructional and professional standards. © 2025 IEEE.
