AB  - Modern enterprise software development is often hampered by the immense complexity of large-scale, proprietary codebases. While Large Language Models (LLMs) offer a path to automate complex software development tasks, their application is frequently limited by context window sizes and critical security concerns over sending source code to external APIs. In this paper, we present a novel, local-first AI agent designed to integrate into modern Integrated Development Environments (IDEs) and build a deep, contextual understanding of codebases while preserving data privacy. The cornerstone of our system is a hybrid knowledge graph that synergistically combines a structured, AI-generated symbolic representation of the code with a dense semantic vector index, all running on the developer's local machine. We introduce a distributed worker architecture that parallelizes the expensive analysis and indexing process across multiple worker processes. Our evaluation demonstrates that this parallel architecture significantly reduces analysis time compared to sequential methods. Furthermore, we show that our hybrid retrieval method provides more accurate and relevant context for downstream code generation tasks than either purely symbolic or purely semantic approaches. Our work presents a practical and secure architecture for embedding powerful, context-aware AI assistants directly within an enterprise development environment, enabling advanced task automation without compromising code privacy. © 2025 IEEE.
