AB  - Large language model (LLM) based code completion tools have greatly enhanced software development productivity by providing repository-aware suggestions. However, existing state-of-the-art decoder-only LLMs, such as Qwen2.5-Coder-3B, suffer from high latency, particularly due to expensive prefill computations when leveraging long, repository-scale contexts. This latency limits their practical deployment in highly interactive programming environments. In this work, we propose an efficient Fusion-in-Decoder (FiD) architecture that extends a pre-trained, high-quality decoder-only code LM with a lightweight encoder and trainable cross-attention layers, while keeping the decoder frozen. Our approach enables independent encoding and caching of cross-file code chunks, substantially reducing prefill time. We design a two-stage data preparation pipeline leveraging repository-level chunking and BM25-based retrieval to supply realistic multi-file context. Experiments on the Long Code Arena and RepoEval-Updated benchmarks demonstrate that our model achieves significant reductions in end-to-end latency, particularly with long prompts, while sustaining strong code completion performance. Our results highlight the promise of FiD-augmented decoder-only models for low-latency cross-file code completion at scale. © 2025 IEEE.
